  "opennyai_summary": {
    "PREAMBLE": "C482/1132/2014 on 16 November, 2019\nAuthor: Alok Singh\nBench: Alok Singh\nC 482 No. 1132 of 2014\nHon'ble Alok Singh, J.\nMr. Ramji Srivastava, Advocate for the petitioner.\nMr. V.K. Gemini, Deputy Advocate General for the State of Uttarakhand.\n",
    "facts": "On 25.02.2010, respondent no. 2 took three packets of bread and sent to the Public Analyst.\nThe Public Analyst reported that last consumption date or expiry date of product is not mentioned on the product, therefore, product is misbranded.\nThereafter, respondent no. 2 after completing all the formalities filed a complaint in the court of Judicial Magistrate, Chamoli.\nLearned Judicial Magistrate, Chamoli vide order dated 17.02.2011 summoned the petitioner to face trial under Section 7/16 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954.\n",
    "arguments": "Rule 32 (g) provides that label of commodity must contain by mentioning 'use by date/recommended last consumption date / expiry date' but the same are in respect of only to the commodities like aspartame and infant milk.\nTherefore, bread is not a commodity which is used after three months, therefore, consumption date / expiry date is not required to be mentioned.\n",
    "ANALYSIS": "In view of the view of the above, I do not find any illegality in the impugned orders.\n",
    "decision": "Therefore,\npresent petition fails and is hereby dismissed.\n"
  },
